# Object translation delta (header `Field Label (EN)` + new-field only)

English for object/field labels is the column whose header is **`Field Label (EN)`**
(JP `項目ラベル名 (EN)`). Locate it by **header name** (same as `WIP`, `IsDelete`,
`fullName`). Untranslated tabs have no `Field Label (EN)` header; do not invent
one or treat `fullName` as English. Object-level English is row 1 of that same
column (`fetch_sheet.py` via `col_map`).

Live Data Dictionary:
https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1_TaxDe-Qxl8BAUmuZc01vUoxpBEPxJ4Opx4tEe8ulNQ

Japanese `Field Label` remains `CustomField.label`. Do **not** write `Field Label (EN)`
into `generate_xml.py` field `<label>`.

`MISSING_TRANSLATION` (blank EN cell) and `SCHEMA_MISSING` (the EN targets a
field that is neither in the org nor created by this deploy) are DIFFERENT
defects and must be reported as such; neither is ever packaged.

## No EN header ⇒ the whole translation step is SKIPPED (mandatory)

A tab with no `Field Label (EN)` column is untranslated, so the translation
step must add **zero** dependencies to that deploy: no catalog entries, no org
session, no Metadata API call. `fetch_sheet.py` stamps every row with
`_HasFieldLabelEN`; `has_translation_columns()` gates Step 4b, and
`entries_from_object_rows()` drops rows from untranslated tabs. Never let an
ordinary field deploy start needing the Translation Workbench.

## Every object/field deploy (not a separate project)

Translations are part of the ONE canonical command — there is no separate
operator step:

```
python scripts/prep_deploy.py --org "<ORG>" --tabs "<TABS>" --phase deploy
```

The translation delta is computed from the **single org snapshot**
(`.build/org_snapshot.json`) that the orchestrator already took for the
object/field delta, and it is recorded in the **deployment plan**
(`.build/deploy_plan.json`) alongside the field members:

1. `org_snapshot.py` reads every target object's `CustomObjectTranslation` ONCE
 in bulk (`ceil(N/10)` readMetadata calls for N objects) and keeps the raw
 record XML.
2. `plan_deploy.py` classifies the sheet's EN against that snapshot and writes
 the `CustomObjectTranslation:<Obj>__c-<LANG>` members into the plan.
3. `generate_object_translation.py --snapshot … --delta <plan>` patches the new
 field into the org's OWN element tree — **no second org read per object**
 (that duplication was ~`ceil(N/10) + N` calls) — and stages the result under
 `.build/staging/force-app`.
4. The COT member rides in the **same** plan-built package as the CustomField
 delta, and its hash is recorded in the sync state **only after**
 `verify_deploy.py` confirms the deploy landed.

`--lang` defaults to `en_US` and is a `prep_deploy.py` flag (`--lang off` skips
translations entirely). Only the selected language directory
(`objectTranslations/<Obj>__c-<LANG>`) is regenerated — other languages staged
in the tree are left untouched.

| Code | When | Package? |
|---|---|---|
| `NEW_TRANSLATION` | EN filled, no `<LANG>` translation in the org | **Yes — automatic** |
| `CHANGED_EN` | Org EN ≠ sheet EN | **Report only.** Redeploy only if the user asks. |
| `MISSING_TRANSLATION` | Deployable custom field, EN blank | WARN; deploy JA field; **do not invent English** |
| `SCHEMA_MISSING` | EN targets a field absent from the org AND from this deploy | **No.** The COT member would fail on an unknown field — report it. |
| `PARSE_ERROR` | EN cell cannot be parsed (e.g. picklist EN count ≠ JA count) | **No — validation ERROR.** Fix the sheet; never silently degrade to MISSING. |
| `INVALID_LANG` | Language code is not a Translation Workbench code | **No — validation ERROR.** |

Honor WIP / IsDelete / `END[項目]` like every other field row. Blank EN is a WARN,
not a Step 0 hard blocker.

## PATCH, never rebuild — a COT deploys as the WHOLE object-language pair

Anything missing from the file we write is **erased** in the org. A
`CustomObjectTranslation` carries far more than field labels: `recordTypes`,
`layouts`, `validationRules`, `fieldSets`, `quickActions`, `webLinks`,
`sharingReasons`, `workflowTasks`, `gender` / `startsWith`, and the plural /
case / possessive `caseValues` variants.

So the generator MUST keep the org's element tree and patch only the nodes the
delta marks new (`split_object_translation` → patch → `render_metadata`):

- every unmentioned parent node is written back verbatim,
- every already-translated field keeps its own `<Field>.fieldTranslation-meta.xml`,
- only the intended field file (and, if changed, the base singular `caseValues`)
  is modified.

Never rebuild the parent file from a reduced model of field labels.

## Standard `Name` is the parent `<nameFieldLabel>`

The standard Name field is translated by the PARENT
`CustomObjectTranslation/<nameFieldLabel>` — **not** by a
`Name.fieldTranslation-meta.xml`. Read `Name Field Label (EN)` from the
object-meta row, parse the org's existing `<nameFieldLabel>`, and write it back
on the parent. Never emit a `Name` field translation file.

## Auth + state are machine- and sandbox-agnostic

- **Auth:** sessions come from the supported CLI (`sf org display --verbose
  --json --target-org <ORG>`). Never decrypt the CLI keychain, never assume
  `~/.sfdx`, `~/.local/share/sf/...`, or a rewritten `HOME`. `prep_deploy.py`
  inherits the environment by default; `--sf-home` / `--xdg-data-home` are
  opt-in sandbox overrides.
- **Preflight:** if the org cannot serve translations for `<LANG>` (Translation
  Workbench off, language not activated), the tools raise an actionable error
  naming Setup → Translation Language Settings. `--on-unavailable skip`
  (`prep_deploy.py --on-translation-unavailable skip`) is the explicit opt-out
  that deploys the fields without translations.
- **State:** `.build/translation_sync_state.json` is keyed by target **org Id**,
  never by alias, so one sandbox's hashes can never mark another sandbox's
  translation "unchanged".

## Future field add (Japan team)

Sheet row: JA in `Field Label`, EN in `Field Label (EN)`, API in `fullName`.
Next `prep_deploy.py` for that tab: field name-delta creates the CustomField;
`--new-only` marks only that row `NEW_TRANSLATION`; generate patches it into the
org COT. Existing translations stay as they are.
